    "full_prompt": "The source material provided for this request follows.\n\n[Continues under: Bowne Publishing in New]\n\n72 a plain english handbook Premium Cigars International, cover page After Only key information on page Offering information is clear and easy to read.\n\nAmple white space is visually inviting and highlights key information.\n\nKey information, like price per share, is highlighted.\n\nLeft justified text eliminates block-like appearance.\n\nTwo-column format is easier to read than text running across page.\n\nDesign and layout highlight market and trading symbols.\n\nShort sentences use concrete, everyday terms.\n\nLanguage is free from legalese, technical language, and unnecessary cross references.\n\nPersonal pronouns, while not required, enhance readability .\n\nBefore General Motors Corporation, summary\n\nLegalese and technical terms, like pursuant to the terms and conditions set forth, and effected largely pursuant to transactions, etc.\n\nCross reference to definition disrupts flow of information; meanings of terms in summary must be clear from context.\n\nTerms like certain and appropriate are vague, not concrete.\n\nPassive voice–who is completing these transactions?\n\nParenthetical phrases are legalistic and disrupt flow of information.\n\nLittle “i”s are legalistic; bulleted list would be easier to read.\n\nOrganization of information driven by mechanics of transactions rather than by information shareholders need to make a decision.\n\nTransactions’ effect on share­ holders not discussed until column two.\n\nIs detail about GM amending its certificate of incorporation necessary for the summary?\n\nUsing this material, complete the following request.\n\nI am updating the presentation-controls section of our risk-disclosure policy. Prepare a prospective risk-disclosure policy traceability matrix using only the supplied plain-English handbook annotations for Premium Cigars International’s “After” cover page and General Motors Corporation’s “Before” summary. Limit the policy coverage to cover-page and summary presentation, rather than substantive risk-factor content or issuer compliance findings.\n\nFor each proposed policy provision, include its applicable disclosure location, the named example and a short supporting quotation, the strength and limits of the source support, a prospective review test, the evidence a reviewer would request, and your recommended policy treatment. Distinguish wording the handbook itself expresses as mandatory, techniques explicitly described as optional, and illustrative advice, praise, criticism, or editorial questions. Mandatory phrasing in an annotation must not be presented as proof of a binding securities-law obligation. Split mixed provisions where their source status or scope differs.\n\nDevelop connected controls for information prominence and page design, plain-language drafting, definitions and cross-references, and decision-focused summary organization and detail selection. Reconcile the cross-reference comments across the two examples when drafting the relevant control, rather than assuming they establish a universal prohibition. Preserve the qualification concerning personal pronouns, and assess whether the question about certificate-of-incorporation detail supports a firm exclusion or only an editorial review check. Where a provision applies to both cover pages and summaries, show separate support for each location or identify the broader application as your proposed extension.\n\nUse the source classification and scope mapping to recommend whether each control should become an internal requirement, remain an advisory check, or await a policy decision. These adoption recommendations are analyst judgment; explicitly flag any proposed internal requirement that is stronger than the supplied wording. Review tests must follow the proposed control’s scope and must not introduce unsupported numerical thresholds. Evidence requests are proposed review materials, not documents the excerpt requires anyone to retain.\n\nEnd with a short adoption note tied to the matrix rows, identifying policy choices that need resolution before the affected controls can be used as pass/fail tests. State that legal applicability and conformity of any current issuer disclosure are not determinable from this excerpt; do not turn missing draft evidence into a compliance failure or import unsupplied rules.",
