    "work_product": "risk-disclosure policy traceability matrix",
    "full_prompt": "The source materials provided for this request follow.\n\n[Continues under: The Transitive Vampire]\n\n22 a plain english handbook Bring abstractions down to earth Abstractions abound in the financial industry. What pictures form in your mind when you read these phrases: mutual fund, the Dow Jones Industrial Average, zero coupon bond, call option, or foreign currency trading? Most people don’t have an image in their minds when they hear abstract words like these. And yet, it’s far easier to comprehend a concept or a situation when your mind can form images.\n\nIn a study conducted at Carnegie-Mellon University, a cognitive psychol­ ogist and an English professor discovered that readers faced with com­ plex written information frequently resorted to creating “scenarios” in an effort to understand the text. That is, they of ten made an abstract concept understandable by using it in a hypothetical situation in which people performed actions.\n\nYou can make complex information more understandable by giving your readers an example using one investor . This technique explains why “question and answer” formats often succeed when a narrative, abstract discussion fails.\n\nHere is an example of how this principle can be used to explain an abstract concept—call options: For example, you can buy an option from Mr. Smith that gives you the right to buy 100 shares of stock X from him at $25.00 per share anytime between now and six weeks from now . You believe stock X’s purchase price will go up between now and then. He believes it will stay the same or go down. If you exercise this option before it expires, Mr. Smith must sell you 100 shares of stock X at $25.00 per share, even if the purchase price has gone up. Either way, whether you exercise your option or not, he keeps the money you paid him for the option.\n\n---\n\n[Continues under: Graphics]\n\nbefore $0 $10 $20 $30 $40 $50 $60 1997 Quarterly Sales Revenue in millions 1st Quarter 2nd Quarter 3rd Quarter 4th Quarter $36 $45 $32 $49 $60 $50 $40 $30 $20 $10 $0 1st Quarter 2nd Quarter 3rd Quarter 4th Quarter 1997 Quarterly Sales Revenue in millions 50 a plain english handbook Check proportions of visuals Generally, you should avoid graphics that start at a non-zero baseline, because they distort differences by destroying correct proportions.\n\nCompare these two bar charts to see how the non-zero baseline can mislead the reader as to the magnitude of change from quarter to quarter.\n\nbefore after $25 $30 $35 $40 $45 $50 $55\n\n1997 Quarterly Sales Revenue\n\nQ1 Q2 Q3 Q4\n\n$36 $45 $32 $49 $0 $10 $20 $30 $40 $50 $60\n\n$36 $45 $32 $49 “Extensive studies of annual reports of major corporations in the United States and the United Kingdom have shown that about 25 percent of the graphs they contain are distorted substantially.”\n\nUsing these materials, complete the following request.\n\nI am developing a prospective internal presentation policy for investor risk disclosures. Prepare a risk-disclosure policy traceability matrix using only the supplied handbook excerpts, “Bring abstractions down to earth” on page 22 and “Check proportions of visuals” on page 50. This is policy design, not an assessment of an issuer’s compliance or the legal completeness of a disclosure.\n\nConnect the excerpts into one drafting-and-review process: make an abstract concept understandable, preserve the substance of any explanatory example, and check proportionality if a supporting graphic is used. For each matrix row, include the source page and a short passage locator, the source statement and its evidentiary status, applicability and qualifications, the proposed internal policy control, and the evidence and acceptance test you recommend for reviewing a future draft. Keep handbook guidance, illustrations, and reported research distinguishable from requirements you propose as analyst judgment; mixed classifications are acceptable where a passage serves more than one purpose.\n\nDo not turn investor scenarios or question-and-answer formats into universally mandatory formats. Preserve the qualification in the advice about non-zero baselines. Identify any exception criteria actually supplied; if none are supplied, say so, and distinguish any exception-review process you recommend from the handbook’s own guidance. Do not infer securities-law duties or required filings from these excerpts.\n\nUse the source illustrations to make the proposed controls testable. In the example-integrity row, identify the economic terms, exercise period, and conditional outcomes from the call-option illustration that a clearer rewrite would need to preserve, including the outcome that does not depend on exercise. Do not infer a premium amount or add a payoff calculation.\n\nFor the visual-proportionality control, use the Q3-to-Q4 movement in the 1997 sales charts under “Check proportions of visuals.” Calculate the percentage change in stated revenue and compare it with the percentage change in baseline-subtracted values under each illustrated baseline. Treat those baseline-subtracted values as an analyst-designed proxy for bar height in revenue-axis units, not pixel measurements or a handbook-mandated formula. Show inputs, units, and denominators, then explain what this diagnostic would flag without inventing a materiality threshold. Keep the sales and option illustrations separate; neither establishes facts about a current issuer.\n\nFinally, derive a conditional presentation sign-off gate from the matrix for a future disclosure combining an investor example with a quantitative graphic. Explain why narrative clarity alone would not resolve a visual-proportionality concern, how the gate changes when no graphic is used, and which concerns require revision versus judgment-based review. Identify only the draft materials and supporting evidence needed for these controls, label them as proposed review evidence rather than source-required documents, and leave actual-case readiness undetermined.",
