    "full_prompt": "The source materials provided for this request follow.\n\n[Continues under: Appendix A to Part 30—Interagency Guidelines Establishing Standards for Safety and Soundness]\n\n1. Are commensurate with the types of loans the institution will make and consider the terms and conditions under which they will be made;\n\n2. Consider the nature of the markets in which loans will be made;\n\n3. Provide for consideration, prior to credit commitment, of the borrower's overall financial condition and resources, the financial responsibility of any guarantor, the nature and value of any underlying collateral, and the borrower's character and willingness to repay as agreed;\n\n4. Establish a system of independent, ongoing credit review and appropriate communication to management and to the board of directors;\n\n5. Take adequate account of concentration of credit risk; and\n\n6. Are appropriate to the size of the institution and the nature and scope of its activities.\n\nE. Interest rate exposure. An institution should:\n\n1. Manage interest rate risk in a manner that is appropriate to the size of the institution and the complexity of its assets and liabilities; and\n\n2. Provide for periodic reporting to management and the board of directors regarding interest rate risk with adequate information for management and the board of directors to assess the level of risk.\n\nF. Asset growth. An institution's asset growth should be prudent and consider:\n\n1. The source, volatility and use of the funds that support asset growth;\n\n2. Any increase in credit risk or interest rate risk as a result of growth; and\n\n3. The effect of growth on the institution's capital.\n\n---\n\n[Continues under: Appendix D to Part 30—OCC Guidelines Establishing Heightened Standards for Certain Large Insured National Banks, Insured Federal Savings Associations, and Insured Federal Branches]\n\nC. Exercise Independent Judgment. When providing active oversight under paragraph III.B. of these Guidelines, each member of the board of directors should exercise sound, independent judgment.\n\nD. Include Independent Directors. To promote effective, independent oversight of the covered bank's management, at least two members of the board of directors: 6\n\n6 This provision does not supersede other regulatory requirements regarding the composition of the Board that apply to Federal savings associations. These institutions must continue to comply with such other requirements.\n\n1. Should not be an officer or employee of the parent company or covered bank and has not been an officer or employee of the parent company or covered bank during the previous three years;\n\n2. Should not be a member of the immediate family, as defined in § 225.41(b)(3) of the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System's Regulation Y (12 CFR 225.41(b)(3)), of a person who is, or has been within the last three years, an executive officer of the parent company or covered bank, as defined in § 215.2(e)(1) of Regulation O (12 CFR 215.2(e)(1)); and\n\n3. Should qualify as an independent director under the listing standards of a national securities exchange, as demonstrated to the satisfaction of the OCC.\n\nUsing these materials, complete the following request.\n\nUsing only the supplied Appendix A and Appendix D extracts, provide a prospective compliance-response option brief for a chief risk officer and board. Analyze the conditional situation in which both apply to an institution; do not infer actual coverage from the appendix titles or invent an institution-specific finding.\n\nSet out in a source-linked decision matrix the opening credit-related provisions in Appendix A, interest-rate exposure under E, asset growth under F, and independent judgment and independent directors under Appendix D, C–D. Identify the relevant expectation, the facts needed to evaluate alignment, a conditional gap trigger, and a possible response for every area. Preserve the stated timing, recipients of reporting or communication, proportionality qualifications, and use of “should.” No institution records are supplied, so distinguish an unknown status from a gap that could be established with evidence. Label suggested records and remediation actions as analyst proposals unless the extract expressly specifies them.\n\nIn the board analysis, explain how the independent-director test works at both the individual-director and board levels. Preserve the minimum number, conjunctive criteria, lookback periods, OCC satisfaction condition, and federal savings association qualification. Identify what cannot be resolved without the referenced definitions, listing standards, or other requirements; their contents and the missing active-oversight paragraph must not be reconstructed. Distinguish independent credit review, each board member’s independent judgment, and qualification as an independent director rather than treating them as interchangeable.\n\nUse the matrix to compare an operational-control response focused on credit, interest-rate risk and growth; a board-oversight response focused on judgment and composition; and a coordinated response. For each, explain which potential gaps it addresses, what it leaves unresolved, and what would need to be established before recommending it as sufficient for the identified gaps within this limited review scope. These are response paths, not exemptions from the other provisions. Connect Appendix A’s management and board information needs to the Appendix D oversight provisions: explain how better risk information could support board judgment, why it would not by itself establish director qualification, and why board-composition changes would not by themselves establish adequate operational controls. Identify these implementation connections as analysis rather than additional source requirements.\n\nEnd with a conditional selection rule derived from the matrix that explains how the recommended path changes when evidence establishes operational gaps, governance gaps, both, or leaves either area unresolved. Specify the evidence that would permit reassessment or closure of the identified issue, without asserting that any option is currently warranted or that this limited analysis establishes overall regulatory compliance.",
