    "full_prompt": "The source material provided for this request follows.\n\n[Continues under: On Writing Well]\n\nUse defined terms sparingly Although customary, introducing defined terms on the cover page and in the summary discourages many readers from getting beyond the first pages. Overwhelmed with memorizing a new and unnatural vocabulary, and bothered by constantly having to flip back and hunt for the first time a defined term’s definition appears, many an investor will not stick with the document. One plain English expert has advised, don’t let a shortcut for the writer become a roadblock for the reader. • a plain english handbook 13 14 a plain english handbook Reorganizing the\n\nDocument\n\nA few principles of good organization apply universally.\n\nFirst, present the big picture before the details. Prospectuses routinely start with a detailed description of the securities. Y ou may read pages before you find out what the company produces, or why it is merging or spinning off a subsidiary. It’s hard to absorb the details if you don’t know why they are being given to you. Imagine trying to put together a complicated jigsaw puzzle without first seeing the picture of the com­ pleted puzzle. An individual piece of information means more to your readers if they know how it fits into the big picture.\n\nSecond, use descriptive headers and subheaders to break your document up into manageable sections. Prospectuses impart a lot of information. If you present the information in bite-sized pieces, it’s easier to digest. M ake sure your headings tell the reader what the upcoming sections will cover. Headings like “general” or “background” aren’t especially helpful.\n\nThird, always group related information together. This helps you identify and eliminate repetitious information.\n\nUsing this material, complete the following request.\n\nUsing only the supplied Plain English Handbook excerpt, provide a source-grounded disclosure compliance brief for a prospectus drafting team. Present it as a prospective drafting-review standard rather than an issuer-specific finding: no draft prospectus or issuer facts have been supplied.\n\nMap the defined-term advice and the document-organization principles into a compact review table. Include, for every entry, a short quoted source anchor, the scope and strength of the source language, a concrete drafting check, and the draft material needed to perform it. Distinguish the handbook's recommendations from your proposed implementation tests, and do not treat imperative editorial language as evidence of a binding securities-law duty. Preserve the distinction between advice applying generally and concerns directed specifically at the opening portions of the document.\n\nDrawing on that mapping, develop a recommended opening-section outline and an approach to organizing related detail later in the prospectus. Use content placeholders rather than invented business or transaction facts. Explain how the proposed structure reconciles giving readers early orientation with grouping related information and reducing repetition, and how the defined-term advice affects the opening. Identify any balancing decisions or section-order choices that are your editorial judgment rather than a format prescribed by the excerpt. Do not turn advice to use defined terms sparingly into a blanket prohibition.\n\nEnd with conditional review outcomes tied to the checks in your table: what draft evidence would support recommending a revision, what would support no revision on that particular issue, and what must remain unassessed until the draft is available. Use qualitative judgments where the source supplies no measurable cutoff rather than inventing numerical limits. Keep evidence requests confined to these drafting checks and label them as proposed review materials, not source-required filings. Explain the limits of any favorable review result: it would address alignment with the supplied guidance, not establish compliance with disclosure requirements absent from the excerpt.",
