    "workflow": "Generated test",
    "task": "Control design alternative comparison and cross-section analysis",
    "work_product": "control-design alternative matrix",
    "full_prompt": "The source material provided for this request follows.\n\n[Continues under: Appendix C to Part 30—OCC Guidelines Establishing Standards for Residential Mortgage Lending Practices]\n\n4. Prepayment penalties that are not limited to the early years of the loan, particularly in subprime loans.\n\n5. Interest rate increases upon default at a level not commensurate with risk mitigation.\n\n6. Call provisions permitting the national bank or Federal savings association to accelerate payment of the loan under circumstances other than the borrower's default under the credit agreement or to mitigate the bank's or savings association's exposure to loss.\n\n7. Absence of an appropriate assessment and documentation of the consumer's ability to repay the loan in accordance with its terms, commensurate with the type of loan, as required by appendix A of this part.\n\n8. Mandatory arbitration clauses or agreements, particularly if the eligibility of the loan for purchase in the secondary market is thereby impaired.\n\n9. Pricing terms that result in the loan's being subject to the provisions of the Home Ownership and Equity Protection Act. 15 U.S.C. 1639 et seq.\n\n10. Original principal balance of the loan in excess of appraised value.\n\n11. Payment schedules that consolidate more than two periodic payments and pay them in advance from the loan proceeds.\n\n12. Payments to home improvement contractors under a home improvement contract from the proceeds of a residential mortgage loan other than by an instrument payable to the consumer, jointly to the consumer and the contractor, or through an independent third party escrow agent.\n\nUsing this material, complete the following request.\n\nFor a residential mortgage risk committee choosing between two prospective approaches—(A) automated origination and funding screens with exception escalation, and (B) structured specialist review before closing or disbursement—prepare a control-design alternative matrix. Base the comparison between them solely on items 4–7 and 10–12 of the supplied Appendix C excerpt. No loan files or evidence of an institution’s existing controls are available.\n\nMake the following visible in the matrix for every selected item:\n- The source condition and its qualifications, exceptions, or permitted circumstances, with the numbered-item reference.\n- Whether evaluating it is data-testable, judgment-dependent, or unresolved without a definition or standard absent from the excerpt. Allow overlapping classifications where appropriate.\n- How each alternative would operate, the proposed evidence inputs and responsible review function, and a material limitation of each design.\n- Conditional handling of a flagged, not-flagged, or unresolved screening result. These statuses must concern the particular source condition, not imply a legal violation or overall loan approval.\n\nPreserve the distinction between a condition that can be detected from contractual or transaction data and a conclusion that requires substantive assessment. A numerical meaning for an undefined term must not be invented, and Appendix A must not be reconstructed from its citation. Distinguish documentation expressly called for in the excerpt from records you recommend collecting to make a control operable. Label proposed holds, escalation routes, ownership, and release criteria as design judgments rather than OCC requirements.\n\nThen validate the proposed automated logic for the principal-versus-appraised-value condition and the payment-consolidation condition. Explain how it treats equality at the stated comparison boundary and preserves any conjunctive conditions; do not invent loan amounts. Apply this validation to the relevant matrix recommendations, revising them if a simple threshold screen would overflag or miss the listed practice.\n\nConclude with a recommended architecture by control area, allowing a hybrid. Use the matrix’s assessment of testability, judgment needs, and unresolved inputs as the basis for the recommendation. Explain which evidence can be shared across contract-term, repayment-assessment, and disbursement controls without allowing one test to substitute for another. Identify the policy clarifications or proposed evidence needed before your recommended design could be accepted for implementation. Do not infer that inclusion in this partial list establishes an outright prohibition or that any actual control deficiency has occurred.",
