    "full_prompt": "The source material provided for this request follows.\n\n[Continues under: Appendix C to Part 30—OCC Guidelines Establishing Standards for Residential Mortgage Lending Practices]\n\nC. Relationship to Other Legal Requirements. Actions by a national bank or Federal savings association in connection with residential mortgage lending that are inconsistent with these Guidelines or Appendix A to this part 30 may also constitute unsafe or unsound practices for purposes of section 8 of the Federal Deposit Insurance Act, 12 U.S.C. 1818, unfair or deceptive practices for purposes of section 5 of the FTC Act, 15 U.S.C. 45, and the OCC's Lending Rules, 12 CFR 34.3 (Lending Rules) and Real Estate Lending Standards, 12 CFR part 34, subpart D, in the case of national banks, and 12 CFR 160.100 and 160.101, in the case of Federal savings associations, or violations of the ECOA and FHA.\n\n1. Except as modified in these Guidelines, or unless the context otherwise requires, the terms used in these Guidelines have the same meanings as set forth in sections 3 and 39 of the Federal Deposit Insurance Act, 12 U.S.C. 1813 and 1831p-1.\n\n2. For purposes of these Guidelines, the following definitions apply:\n\na. Residential mortgage loan means any loan or other extension of credit made to one or more individuals for personal, family, or household purposes secured by an owner-occupied 1-4 family residential dwelling, including a cooperative unit or mobile home.\n\nUsing this material, complete the following request.\n\nProvide compliance counsel designing a residential-mortgage review for a national bank or Federal savings association with a regulatory threshold-condition brief. Rely solely on the supplied Appendix C excerpt. Present this as a prospective screening framework rather than a finding about any actual loan or institution.\n\nBegin with a decision table that separates institution scope from the definition of “residential mortgage loan” in paragraph 2(a). Break the definition into cumulative conditions and alternatives within those conditions, preserving the dwelling-unit range and express inclusions. Explain whether those inclusions remove any other definitional conditions. Provide decision rules for classifying a future transaction as within the supplied definition, outside it, or undetermined: distinguish a demonstrated failure of a required condition from missing evidence. Falling outside this definition must not be equated with exemption from the other laws referenced in C. Explain how paragraph 1’s incorporation of statutory meanings interacts with the express definition, retaining both qualifications to that incorporation without inventing the missing statutory definitions.\n\nUse the scope screen to organize a conditional map of C’s relationship to other legal requirements. Distinguish references shared by both institution types from the institution-specific regulatory references, and retain the citations supplied in the excerpt without adding citations for laws named only by acronym. Keep the predicate—actions in connection with residential mortgage lending that are inconsistent with these Guidelines or Appendix A—separate from the possible additional legal consequences. Explain why satisfying the loan definition does not establish inconsistency, and preserve the significance of “may also” rather than treating inconsistency as an automatic finding under each referenced authority.\n\nEnd with a decision-and-evidence section keyed to those screening steps. State what the excerpt establishes, what remains conditional, and what narrow factual evidence or missing source provisions would be needed before making transaction- or institution-specific determinations. Limit this gap analysis to institution scope, the loan-definition conditions, the inconsistency predicate, and the additional legal consequences identified in C. Clearly distinguish analyst-proposed evidence from any documents expressly required by the supplied text; do not infer substantive legal tests, operational duties, or notification requirements from citations alone. Place references to C, paragraph 1, or paragraph 2(a) beside the material rules and qualifications so counsel can trace the analysis.",
