    "work_product": "risk-disclosure evidence specification brief",
    "full_prompt": "The source materials provided for this request follow.\n\nRobin Williams, The Mac Is Not a T ypewriter\n\nFranklin Gothic Condensed\n\nJustified text was the style for many years—we grew up on it.\n\nBut there has been a great deal of research on readability (how easy something is to read) and it shows that those disruptive, inconsistent gaps between the words inhibit the flow of reading. Besides, they look dumb. Keep your eyes open as you look at professionally-printed work...and you’ll find there’s a very strong trend now to align type on the left and leave the right ragged.\n\nRobin Williams, The Mac Is Not a Typewriter\n\nYou can mix different typefaces, but do so with discretion; not all type­ faces work well together. Mixing a serif and sans serif, as we have done in this handbook, can look good and create a clear contrast between your levels. Mixing two serif or two sans serif typefaces can look like a mistake. As a general rule, do not use more than two typefaces in any document, not including the bold or italic versions of a typeface.\n\n---\n\nEntire Prospectus—Design\n\nIn designing the entire prospectus:\n\n• may use pictures, logos, charts, graphs, or other design elements\n\n• encouraged to use tables, schedules, charts, and graphics for f inancial data\n\n• must draw graphs and charts to scale\n\n• cannot use misleading design and information a plain english handbook 65\n\nPresentation of information in prospectuses\n\n(b) You must present the information in a prospectus in a clear, concise and understandable manner . You must prepare the prospectus using the following standards: ( ) Present information in clear, concise sections, paragraphs, and sentences. Whenever possible, use short, explanatory sentences and bullet lists;\n\n(2) Use descriptive headings and subheadings;\n\n(3) Avoid frequent reliance on glossaries or defined terms as the primary means of explaining information in the prospectus.\n\nDefine terms in a glossary or other section of the document only if the meaning is unclear from the context. Use a glossary only if it facilitates understanding of the disclosure; and\n\n(4) Avoid legal and highly technical business terminology.\n\nNote to §230.421(b): In drafting the disclosure to comply with this section, you should avoid the following: . Legalistic or overly complex presentations that make the substance of the disclosure difficult to understand;\n\n2. Vague “boilerplate” explanations that are imprecise and readily subject to different interpretations;\n\n3. Complex information copied directly from legal documents without any clear and concise explanation of the provision(s); and\n\n4. Disclosure repeated in different sections of the document that increases the size of the document but does not enhance the quality of the information.\n\nUsing these materials, complete the following request.\n\nProvide a risk-disclosure evidence specification brief for a prospectus review team on the assumption that the supplied presentation provisions apply. We have the excerpts but no issuer draft, rendered pages or supporting chart data, so this must be a prospective review specification—not a finding that any disclosure complies or fails. Limit the review to presentation; do not assess substantive risk-factor completeness or import other securities-law requirements.\n\nSet out explanatory drafting and terminology, headings and visual hierarchy, glossary use, and graphics in a practical review matrix. Identify a short source anchor, its scope, the force of the source wording, the evidence a reviewer should request, and the acceptance condition for every test. Distinguish express duties and prohibitions from permissions, encouragement and advice; preserve qualifications such as conditional glossary use and the circumstances attached to drafting recommendations. Keep the presentation provision, its Note, the whole-prospectus design summary and the typography guidance identifiable rather than treating them as interchangeable legal authority. Label proposed review artifacts as analyst-selected evidence unless the excerpts expressly require them.\n\nIntegrate the alignment and typeface guidance into the evidence requests for readability and hierarchy. Explain how those inspections support the clear, understandable and nonmisleading presentation review without making a departure from typography advice an automatic compliance failure. Preserve the qualifications and exclusions in the typeface guidance. A reviewer should be able to distinguish an advisory design concern from a potential issue under an express presentation duty and identify what further evidence would be needed to establish the latter.\n\nGraphic-related checks must be conditional on the presence of the relevant elements. Distinguish failure to use an optional or encouraged visual from failure to satisfy a requirement governing a visual that is used. Specify how rendered charts or graphs and their underlying data would support a scale and misleading-presentation check, without inventing numerical tolerances or attempting calculations from absent values.\n\nUse the source classifications and evidence dependencies to define review dispositions: what could support acceptance, what would require correction if demonstrated, what would remain undetermined pending evidence, and what would be advisory only. Separate missing evidence from proven failure. Bound evidence gaps to the tests in this brief, and distinguish checks possible on the risk-disclosure pages from those needing other prospectus sections or the complete rendered document, including checks for unhelpful repetition. Close by providing a conditional readiness gate that identifies the evidence needed for a risk-disclosure presentation conclusion and the limits of any conclusion about the prospectus as a whole.",
