    "full_prompt": "The source materials provided for this request follow.\n\n4.4 Late Fees ..................................................................................................11\n4.5 Charge-Offs............................................................................................. 12\n5. Conclusion.........................................................................................................14\n6. Appendix............................................................................................................153 CONSUMER FINANCIAL PROTECTION BUREAU\n1. Introduction\nThe market for buy now, pay later (BNPL) credit, typically a four-payment loan with no interest\nused by consumers to make retail purchases, continues to expand.1 Using data provided by six\nlarge BNPL companies,2 we examine market trends and key metrics in the BNPL market for\ncalendar years 2022 and 2023. The data demonstrates that BNPL products remain a popular\nfinancing choice with consumers.3\nfurther public understanding of the BNPL market as part of the CFPB’s obligation to collect,\nresearch, monitor, and publish information relevant to the functioning of markets for consumer\nfinancial products and services.4 It builds upon our prior BNPL work, including our 2022 report\non BNPL market metrics and practices (hereinafter “2022 Market Trends report”),5 a report\nexploring the financial profiles of BNPL users as part of our Making Ends Meet survey,6 and a\nreport on BNPL usage that found that 21 percent of consumers with a credit record made at least\none purchase using BNPL and that included findings on frequency of use, simultaneous BNPL\nloans, and the other credit characteristics of BNPL users.7\nCurrently, there is only minimal public data on the size of the BNPL market or market trends.\nThe CFPB, however, was able to gather adequate data about BNPL products that enable us to\nprovide general information about BNPL market trends.\n\n---\n\n2022), https://www.consumerfinance.gov/data-research/research-reports/buy-now-pay-later-market-trends-and-consumer-impacts/.\n6 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, Consumer Use of Buy Now, Pay Later (March 2023),\nhttps://files.consumerfinance.gov/f/documents/cfpb_consumer-use-of-buy-now-pay-later_2023-03.pdf.\n7 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, Consumer Use of Buy Now, Pay Later and Other Unsecured Debt (January\n2025), https://files.consumerfinance.gov/f/documents/cfpb_BNPL_Report_2025_01.pdf.4 CONSUMER FINANCIAL PROTECTION BUREAU\nfrom other products that the companies may offer, including products that the companies and\nothers may characterize as BNPL. 5 CONSUMER FINANCIAL PROTECTION BUREAU\n2. Key Findings\nThe following trends were observed from the data collected from six large BNPL firms:\n• The volume of BNPL loans continues to grow, albeit at a slower pace than in\nprevious years. From 2022 to 2023, the number of loans made by the lenders we\nsurveyed increased by 23 percent, and the total dollar amount of loans originated\nincreased by 26 percent when adjusted for inflation, representing slower rates of growth\nthan in prior years (see Table 1).\n• The number of BNPL users has expanded in recent years. The six firms in our\nsample reported a combined 53.6 million consumers who took out at least one BNPL\nloan in 2023, a 12 percent increase from 2022 (see Table 2). Since lenders do not\nnecessarily know if a user has BNPL loans from other lenders, these metrics do not\naccount for users who may have taken a BNPL loan across multiple lenders and thus\nlikely overestimate the number of BNPL users.\n\nUsing these materials, complete the following request.\n\nDevelop a BNPL market analysis brief for our consumer-credit strategy committee, relying solely on the supplied CFPB excerpts. We need defensible language for an executive presentation about 2022–2023 expansion, including whether the evidence supports claims of broader consumer adoption or deeper usage.\n\nEstablish the evidence boundary first: describe the product as qualified in the Introduction, identify the provider sample and calendar periods, and distinguish findings for the sampled firms from estimates of the overall market. Every product marketed as BNPL must not be assumed to be covered.\n\nBuild a compact quantitative table that:\n- Estimates the combined lender-reported consumer total for 2022 by reversing the stated growth to 2023.\n- Indexes loan count, inflation-adjusted originated dollars, and the combined reported-consumer total to 2022 = 100.\n- Uses those indices to calculate the percentage changes in average real originated dollars per loan and loans per unit of the combined reported-consumer count.\nShow the formulas and units, label calculations as analyst-derived approximations because the published inputs are rounded, and retain the inflation-adjusted basis of the dollar measure. Absolute loan counts, dollar volumes, or earlier growth rates must not be invented from the references to missing tables.\n\nReconcile the Introduction’s 21 percent usage statistic with the consumer total in Key Findings before deciding whether they can support a common penetration estimate or an implied population size. Identify the population, denominator or counting basis, study attribution, and available timing for each measure. Keep publication dates separate from observation periods and mark missing timing as unknown. Explain how possible cross-lender duplication affects the interpretation of both the consumer total and the calculated loans-per-reported-consumer measure. If the two statistics are not demonstrably comparable, leave the combined estimate uncalculated and explain why.\n\nUsing that analysis, distinguish directly reported findings, analyst-derived aggregate results, and conclusions not established about activity growth, average real loan size, unique-consumer adoption, and increased borrowing by the same individuals. Preserve the report’s qualitative statement about slower growth without presenting it as an independently verified deceleration calculation. Do not infer borrower-level behavior or causality from aggregate ratios. Support material figures and judgments with references to the relevant supplied section or footnote; linked studies and referenced tables are not evidence beyond what the excerpts actually disclose.\n\nConclude with proposed executive-slide wording that reflects those distinctions. For any unresolved conclusions about unique reach or individual usage, recommend targeted additional data and explain which conclusion each item would help test. Present these requests as analyst recommendations, not CFPB reporting requirements.",
