    "task": "Policy requirement traceability across disclosure provisions",
    "work_product": "risk-disclosure policy traceability matrix",
    "full_prompt": "The source materials provided for this request follow.\n\nHorner/Webb/Miller\n\nHarbrace College Handbook\n\nKeep your sentence structure parallel A long sentence often fails without a parallel structure. Parallelism simply means ensuring a list or series of items is presented using parallel parts of speech, such as nouns or verbs. Note the quotation in the margin.\n\nIn this section, we’ve shown each parallel structure we’ve used in bold.\n\nHere’s an example from a mutual fund prospectus that lacks parallel structure: before If you want to buy shares in Fund X by mail, fill out and sign the Account Application form, making your check payable to “The X Fund,” and put your social security or taxpayer identification number on your check.\n\nIf you want to buy shares in Fund X by mail, fill out and sign the Account Application form, make your check payable to “The X Fund,” and put your social security or taxpayer identification number on your check.\n\nHere is a more subtle example from another mutual fund prospectus: before We invest the Fund’s assets in short-term money market securities to provide you with liquidity, protection of your investment, and high current income.\n\nThis sentence is unparallel because its series is made up of two nouns and an adjective before the third noun. It’s also awkward because the verb provide is too closely paired with the nominalization protection.\n\nOne logical revision to the original sentence is to change the noun series to a verb series.\n\nWe invest in short-term money market securities to provide you with liquidity, to protect your investment, and to generate high current income.\n\n---\n\n[Continues under: Plain English Examples]\n\nExamples for illustration purposes only We are providing these examples and marginal notes only to illustrate the principles of plain English. We have not made any determination as to whether the filings are accurate or complete. You are responsible for the substance in your plain English filings. To understand the plain English rules fully, you must read the proposing and adopting releases:\n\n• the proposing release—Release no. 33-7380; and\n\n• the adopting release—Release no. 33-7497.\n\nBoth releases are available from the SEC’s Public Reference Room and our web site at www .sec.gov.\n\nWe collected many filings from the Division’s plain English pilot in one book.\n\nYou can get a photocopy of this 756-page book, Plain\n\nEnglish Pilot Program: Selected\n\nPlain English Samples, from\n\nthe SEC’s Public Reference Room or a printed copy from\n\nBowne Publishing in New\n\nYork City. (The SEC does not endorse Bowne, nor do we have a business relationship with them.)\n\na plain english handbook 69 Before MBNA cover page, core prospectus Fully-justified text and lengthy paragraphs give page a dense, block-like appearance.\n\nAbstract terms and legal jargon prevalent A lot of passive voice, adding to sentence length Eliminate defined terms from the cover page.\n\nLong sentence–60+ words Why capitalize these common terms?\n\nLong sentence–50+ words Many long sentences– 40+ words Centered text and all capital letters are hard to read.\n\nLegalistic tone–does Item 501 require all this information?\n\nUsing these materials, complete the following request.\n\nThe disclosure committee is developing a prospective internal policy for drafting and reviewing risk disclosures. Using only the supplied handbook extracts, prepare a risk-disclosure policy traceability matrix. The committee may adopt editorial standards as internal policy choices, but do not present the extracts as risk-factor-specific legal requirements or as evidence that any actual filing complies.\n\nFor each matrix entry, show an identifiable source passage with a short quotation, its original scope or condition, its source function, the proposed internal control or unresolved dependency, and the conclusion a reviewer could legitimately draw. Distinguish editorial guidance, illustrative examples or commentary, explicit handbook instructions or responsibility statements, and references to unsupplied authority. Allow overlapping functions where appropriate, and label proposed controls as analyst recommendations rather than source-imposed requirements.\n\nTrace the parallel-structure standard through both mutual-fund examples. Explain the editing principle each illustrates and translate that principle into a conditional check for future risk-disclosure drafts. Do not turn the examples’ purchase instructions or investment language into substantive policy requirements, and do not make a verb series the only acceptable construction simply because one revision uses it.\n\nIntegrate the Plain English Examples passage into the same control framework. State the scope of its illustration-only caveat, and connect the filer’s responsibility for substance to the limits of any presentation-review conclusion. Include the MBNA cover-page comments on defined terms and sentence length: distinguish page-specific advice from descriptive observations, do not convert annotated word counts into mandatory limits, and identify any broader application to risk disclosures as a policy choice. Trace the instruction to consult the proposing and adopting releases, identifying both by release number. Preserve the Item 501 remark as a question whose answer is not supplied; do not reconstruct that item or either release from outside knowledge.\n\nAfter the matrix, propose a short acceptance-and-escalation sequence whose branches follow from the source classifications and controls you mapped. Explain what a successful wording check could clear under the proposed internal policy, what would remain unresolved about accuracy, completeness, or legal requirements, and what would be needed before those unresolved matters could be assessed. Separate documents the handbook expressly tells readers to consult from evidence you recommend obtaining. Keep the gap analysis limited to the matrix’s controls and cited authorities, permit conditional or not-determinable outcomes, and do not approve or reject an actual filing.",
    "full_prompt_assembly": "verbatim-natural-bridges-v1",
