    "full_prompt": "The source material provided for this request follows.\n\nAlan J. Davis\n\nGraphical Information: Where to Draw the Line Draw graphics to scale Any graphic should be proportionately correct or drawn to scale.\n\nFor example, if you are showing an increase in oil production through a series of oil barrels in ever-increasing sizes, make sure a barrel isn’t represented as 50% bigger when production only went up 25% that year.\n\nBe consistent when grouping graphics If you group graphics side-by-side, avoid changing your scale from one graphic to another, as in the examples above. Also, lining up three graphics that present data in billions, millions, then dollars can mislead the reader.\n\na plain english handbook 51 Don’t reverse time In graphics showing information over time, time should flow forward, not backward. In these examples, even though the first graphic is clearly labeled, it gives a false visual impression that earnings are going down over time rather than up.\n\nAnnual Sales Revenue\n\nin millions $200 $162 $123 $87 $150 $100 $50 $0 1997 1996 1995\n\nin millions $200 $150 $100 $50 $0 $162 $87 $123 1995 1996 1997 Organize data to hasten insights Choose an organization that helps the reader grasp information and comparisons quickly. For instance, if you have a list of foreign stock markets showing their returns in one year, list them in descending order by the magnitude of their returns instead of in alphabetical order.\n\nUsing this material, complete the following request.\n\nI’m designing a compliance review of graphics used in risk disclosures and have only the supplied plain-English handbook excerpt—not an issuer draft or its underlying data. Provide a risk-disclosure evidence specification brief that a reviewer can use to request evidence and test presentation. The excerpt must be treated as presentation guidance, not as proof of a securities-law obligation or of any issuer’s compliance.\n\nDevelop an evidence-to-test matrix that covers proportional representation of quantities, consistency of scales and units in side-by-side graphics, chronological direction, and organization that helps readers make relevant comparisons. Identify the supporting heading or short passage, the feature that makes the check applicable, the evidence needed to perform it, and the conditions that would support a finding of alignment, inconsistency, or an unresolved assessment for each check. A graphic may trigger several checks; distinguish inapplicability from insufficient evidence. The strength and scope of the handbook’s language must be preserved rather than converting every recommendation into an absolute rule.\n\nMake the proposed tests operational. Explain how the reviewer would connect displayed quantities to underlying data and scaling choices, assess grouped graphics without relying solely on individual labels, and verify time direction. For data ordering, preserve the scope of the single-year stock-market-return example rather than prescribing one sorting method for all graphics. Distinguish source passages supporting a check from documents expressly required by the excerpt and artifacts you recommend obtaining; do not imply that proposed evidence requests are handbook-mandated documents.\n\nValidate the proportionality check using the oil-production illustration: calculate the difference between the stated represented growth and underlying growth in percentage points, and separately calculate the ratio of those growth percentages. Label this as a handbook illustration, not an issuer finding. Explain what those calculations do and do not establish about the graphic’s geometry, without assuming that “bigger” means height, area, or volume. Use that limitation to refine the evidence request for pictorial scaling. Similarly, do not reconstruct visual details of the revenue charts that cannot be established from the supplied text.\n\nFinish with an evidence-request and reassessment sequence derived from the unresolved checks. Explain what each requested item would resolve and which possible findings would call for a graphic revision, additional evidence, or both. Identify this sequence and any proposed acceptance criteria as reviewer judgment, add no unsupported numerical tolerances, and state the limited presentation conclusion that could be reached once sufficient evidence is available. Missing issuer materials must remain an evidence gap, not an adverse issuer finding; the brief must not certify the completeness of substantive risk disclosure.",
