    "full_prompt": "The source material provided for this request follows.\n\n[Continues under: Graphics]\n\nbefore $0 $10 $20 $30 $40 $50 $60 1997 Quarterly Sales Revenue in millions 1st Quarter 2nd Quarter 3rd Quarter 4th Quarter $36 $45 $32 $49 $60 $50 $40 $30 $20 $10 $0 1st Quarter 2nd Quarter 3rd Quarter 4th Quarter 1997 Quarterly Sales Revenue in millions 50 a plain english handbook Check proportions of visuals Generally, you should avoid graphics that start at a non-zero baseline, because they distort differences by destroying correct proportions.\n\nCompare these two bar charts to see how the non-zero baseline can mislead the reader as to the magnitude of change from quarter to quarter.\n\nbefore after $25 $30 $35 $40 $45 $50 $55\n\n1997 Quarterly Sales Revenue\n\nQ1 Q2 Q3 Q4\n\n$36 $45 $32 $49 $0 $10 $20 $30 $40 $50 $60\n\n$36 $45 $32 $49 “Extensive studies of annual reports of major corporations in the United States and the United Kingdom have shown that about 25 percent of the graphs they contain are distorted substantially.”\n\nUsing this material, complete the following request.\n\nProvide our financial-reporting team with a source-grounded disclosure compliance brief for use as a prospective drafting and review standard for quarterly revenue graphics. Rely solely on the supplied plain-English handbook excerpt, and present its charts as illustrations rather than evidence about our company or an existing disclosure draft.\n\nMap the baseline advice to its implications for disclosure drafting, using short supporting quotations or chart labels. Distinguish guidance, illustrative data and reported research, and state whether the excerpt establishes any binding disclosure obligation. Preserve the qualification in the baseline advice rather than converting it into an absolute prohibition. Explain the scope and evidentiary limits of the quoted annual-report study, including whether it supports an acceptance threshold for an individual graphic.\n\nTake the paired “before” and “after” charts under “Check proportions of visuals” as a worked example. Identify their period, units, quarterly amounts and displayed baselines, and check whether the underlying revenue values change between versions. For Q3 to Q4, calculate the actual percentage increase in revenue and the percentage increase in displayed bar length under each baseline. For this analytical comparison, define bar length as revenue minus the displayed baseline; show the workings and do not attribute that formula to the handbook. If a necessary chart detail cannot be read reliably, flag it and leave the affected result conditional rather than inventing a value.\n\nUse the source mapping and numerical comparison to recommend a drafting treatment and practical sign-off criteria covering baseline and scale, visual proportionality, and consistency of the revenue amounts, period and units. Separate source-backed guidance from your proposed internal controls. Explain how a non-zero-baseline draft should be handled under the qualified advice without inventing a formal exception procedure or declaring it automatically unlawful.\n\nEnd with the evidence needed to apply those criteria to an actual draft and the conclusions that remain undetermined without it. Evidence requests must remain within those review fields and be identified as proposed review evidence, not documents expressly required by the excerpt.",
