    "full_prompt": "The source material provided for this request follows.\n\n[Continues under: The Elements of Style]\n\nWe provide information to you about our notes in three separate documents that progressively provide more detail: 1) the prospectus,\n\n2) the prospectus supplement, and 3) the pricing supplement. Since the terms of speciﬁc notes may differ from the general information we have provided, in all cases rely on information in the pricing supplement over different information in the prospectus and the prospectus supplement; and rely on this prospectus supplement over different information in the prospectus.\n\n28 a plain english handbook or We provide information to you about our notes in three separate documents that progressively provide more detail: 1 The Prospectus General information that may or may not apply to each note.\n\n2 The Prospectus Supplement\n\nMore speciﬁc than the prospectus, and to the extent information differs from the prospectus, rely on the different information in this document.\n\n3 The Pricing Supplement\n\nProvides ﬁnal details about a speciﬁc note including its price. To the extent information differs from the prospectus or the prospectus supplement, rely on the different information in this document.\n\nUsing this material, complete the following request.\n\nProvide a source-grounded disclosure compliance brief for a capital-markets drafting team considering the supplied plain-English handbook excerpt as a model for a notes offering. Limit the brief to a prospective drafting standard supported by this passage; do not treat the handbook example as an independent legal obligation or evidence of an issuer’s actual compliance.\n\nRead the paragraph and the alternative numbered presentation together. Create a compact mapping of the content needed to explain the three documents’ roles, applicability to a particular note, and reliance when information differs. Provide, for every mapped point, a short supporting quotation identified by presentation, an indication of whether the point is explicit in both versions or only one, and a checkable acceptance criterion for a future draft. Distinguish substantive disclosure content from presentation choices and from your own drafting recommendations; do not treat the alternatives as cumulative required formats.\n\nRely on that mapping to write a reader-facing replacement disclosure that retains the source-supported distinctions without inventing issuer facts, note terms, or legal requirements. Explain briefly how the draft preserves the limits on the reliance instructions rather than implying that one document replaces every statement in another.\n\nTest the replacement against three hypothetical reviewer questions: which information should a reader rely on if the two supplements differ; which information should the reader rely on if only the prospectus and prospectus supplement differ; and what can be concluded if the pricing supplement is silent on a point addressed elsewhere? For each, connect the conclusion to the mapping and assess whether the replacement communicates the supported result. Present these as hypothetical configurations rather than observed discrepancies, and leave any conclusion unresolved where silence or missing information does not support a reliance decision.\n\nClose with a limited review-readiness assessment. Identify which drafting criteria can be established from the excerpt and which checks require actual offering-document evidence. Tie each proposed evidence request to a mapped criterion, limit the requests to document roles, applicability, and reliance language, and distinguish documents described in the model from any legally mandated document requirement that the excerpt does not establish. Unavailable offering documents must not be labeled as a confirmed compliance failure.",
