    "full_prompt": "The source materials provided for this request follow.\n\nSBA Form 1919 (02/2025) 5 \nPurpose of this form: \nThe purpose of this form is to collect information about the Small Business Applicant (“Applicant”) and its owners, the loan request, existing indebtedness, \ninformation about current or previous government financing, and certain other topics. The information also facilitates background checks as authorized by \nsection 7(a)(1)(B) of the Small Business Act, 15 U.S.C. 636(a)(1)(B). Submission of the requested information is required for SBA or the Lender to \ndetermine eligibility for financial assistance. Failure to submit the information would affect that determination. \nInstructions for completing this form: \nA separate SBA Form 1919 for each co-Applicant(e.g., EPC and OC) must be completed and signed by the authorized representative of the Applicant and \nsubmitted to your SBA Participating Lender. The amounts of the “Purpose of the loan” sections across all SBA Forms 1919 should equal the total amount \nof the loan request. Submission of the requested information is required to make a determination regarding eligibility for financial assistance. \nFor purposes of reporting NAICS Code, Applicants must match the business activity code provided on their IRS income tax filings, if applicable. \nAll parties listed below are considered Owners of the Applicant: \n• For a sole proprietorship, the sole proprietor; \n• For a partnership, all general partners, and all limited partners owning 20% or more of the equity of the firm; \n• For a corporation, all owners of 20% or more of the corporation; \n• For limited liability companies, all members owning 20% or more of the company; and \n• Any Trustor (if the Applicant is owned by a trust).\n\n---\n\n[Continues under: Variations of this document]\n\nVersion\r\n5(A) | Effective: March 1, 2009 |\r\n\r\nFile size: 4524KB | Download .pdf for 5(A) / March 1, 2009\n\nVersion\r\n5 | Effective: August 1, 2008 |\r\n\r\nFile size: 1430KB | Download .pdf for 5 / August 1, 2008\n\nRelated documents\n\nSOP\r\n50 55 504 Loan Servicing and Liquidation\n\n504 Loan Servicing and Liquidation\n\nSOP\r\n50 57 7(a) Loan Servicing and Liquidation\n\n7(a) Loan Servicing and Liquidation\n\nSOP\r\n50 10, Appendix 13 7(a) Manufacturers' Access to Revolving Credit (MARC)\n\n7(a) Manufacturers' Access to Revolving Credit (MARC)\n\nUsing these materials, complete the following request.\n\nUsing only the supplied Form 1919 instructions and SOP document listing, prepare an application evidence and open-items checklist for a prospective SBA loan intake. This packet is not a completed borrower file, so distinguish requirements that can be established now from applicant-specific checks that cannot yet be performed.\n\nPresent each check, its source basis, any applicability condition, evidence expressly required by the excerpt, additional verification evidence you recommend, and the condition for clearing the item in a checklist table. Recommendations must be labeled as reviewer judgment rather than SBA requirements. Distinguish borrower information not supplied, applicability unresolved, and governing text or version unresolved; allow overlapping labels and do not describe these packet limitations as proven applicant failures.\n\nFocus the application review on these connected controls:\n- Establishing the co-Applicant population, then checking separate Form 1919 completion, authorized-representative signatures, and submission to the participating lender. Explain how the co-Applicant inventory determines both the forms to collect and the population used in the loan-purpose reconciliation.\n- Identifying who falls within the excerpt's definition of Owners for each stated entity type, preserving its thresholds and exceptions. An Owner must not be equated with a co-Applicant, and no additional owner signature or separate-form requirement may be inferred.\n- Checking NAICS consistency with the business activity code on IRS income tax filings when applicable. Keep the matching instruction separate from any recommendation to obtain tax documents, and explain how unresolved applicability affects the check.\n- Reconciling the amounts in the Purpose of the loan sections across all relevant Forms 1919 to the total loan request. Identify the necessary inputs and show a symbolic tie-out, but do not supply amounts or claim a numerical result without case data.\n\nThe same checklist must include an authority-control open item. Compare the Form edition with the SOP versions and effective dates actually listed, and assess whether the listing supplies substantive eligibility, underwriting, or approval conditions that can be mapped to application evidence. Do not assume that an older listed version either governs or is inapplicable solely because of its date, and do not infer rule content from related-document titles. Specify the version confirmation and underlying passages you would recommend obtaining before extending the review to those conditions; do not present that recommendation as a required borrower attachment. Limit other evidence-gap checks to the application controls above, rather than inventing fields or attachments from the Form's purpose statement.\n\nClose with a dependency-based collection plan separating borrower evidence requests from source-document requests. Then explain, conditionally, which checklist tests could be performed if completed, signed Forms and a total requested loan amount were supplied, which would still require applicability or other verification, and why that scenario would not by itself resolve any authority-content gap or establish borrower eligibility or approval.",
