    "full_prompt": "The source material provided for this request follows.\n\nCFPB complaints. Most often the reported relief is non-monetary (e.g., an NCRA reporting that\nit made changes to a consumer’s report).\nReporting requirements and scope\nThis report fulfills the FCRA Section 611(e)(5) reporting requirement, which directs the CFPB to\nsubmit to Congress an annual report regarding information gathered by the CFPB.\n10 This report\nsummarizes certain complaints about credit reporting inaccuracies that have been lodged\nagainst the NCRAs—specifically those where the consumer appears to have previously utilized\nFCRA dispute procedures—and the actions taken by NCRAs in response to these complaints.\nThis report proceeds through the following sections. Section 2 summarizes credit and consumer\nreporting complaints overall and situates complaints that are covered by FCRA Section 611(e).\nSection 3 provides more detail on how the NCRAs are responding to covered complaints and\ndescribes the actions they report having taken with respect to these complaints.\nData sources\nThis report uses CFPB complaint data collected during the consumer complaint process. This\nreport focuses primarily on CFPB complaint data from January 2024 through June 2025, but\nincludes data going back to 2020 to provide more context in some figures. The NCRAs have\nadditional response fields that are presented only on certain “covered” issues (see Figure 3).\nThese fields were introduced in 2012 to allow the CFPB to efficiently complete the data\ncollection required by FCRA Section 611(e). The NCRAs’ responses are summarized in section\n2.2 of this report.\n10 15 U.S.C. § 1681i(e)(5). Note the statutory language refers to the Federal Trade Commission (“Commission”) as the\nissuer of this report. However, that is likely a drafting error given the data is collected by the Bureau. 6 CONSUMER FINANCIAL PROTECTION BUREAU\n2. Complaint data\nFrom January 1, 2024, to June 30, 2025, the CFPB received more than 5.6 million complaints.\nMost of these complaints—almost 4.8 million—were about credit and consumer reporting.\n11 And\nmost credit reporting complaints—about 3.9 million—were about the three largest NCRAs\n(Equifax, Experian, and TransUnion). Complaints to NCRAs dominated the overall growth of\ncredit reporting complaints, with an almost 3,000% increase in NCRA complaints since January\n1, 2020.\nConsumers can also submit complaints about furnishers and other, more specialized CRAs.\nSome complaints are forwarded to other regulators because the named company does not\nparticipate in the CFPB’s complaint process (e.g., the company does not offer a consumer\nfinancial product or service, such as a utility company). These categories also increased since\nJanuary 1, 2020, by about 1,000% combined (Figure 2).\n\nUsing this material, complete the following request.\n\nProvide a complaint-scope and limitations brief for a consumer-credit risk committee deciding how to use the supplied CFPB excerpt in a complaint dashboard and management commentary. Rely solely on the excerpt, and present it as aggregate complaint reporting rather than as verified consumer files or findings of legal violations.\n\nEstablish the evidence perimeter first. In a compact table, distinguish all CFPB complaints, credit and consumer reporting complaints, complaints about the three largest NCRAs, and complaints covered by the report’s FCRA Section 611(e) scope. Identify the available volume, its original numerical qualifiers, the applicable period, and the stated basis for inclusion for every population; mark information not supplied as unknown. Explain how the reporting mandate, primary data window, and older contextual data differ. Address the qualification concerning consumers’ previous use of dispute procedures and the selective availability of additional NCRA response fields without assuming that these populations or their denominators are interchangeable.\n\nUsing that scope analysis, assess four proposed dashboard uses: the headline NCRA volume as a count of covered complaints; an exact NCRA share of credit and consumer reporting complaints; comparison of NCRA complaint growth with the combined growth of the other categories discussed; and reported non-monetary relief as a measure of successful resolution. Classify each as Supported, Qualified, or Not established. Supported means the proposed interpretation follows from the excerpt; Qualified means the same measure can be presented only with explicit limitations; Not established means indispensable population, numerical, or outcome evidence is missing. Ground each assessment in a named source section or short supporting passage.\n\nFor the share and growth assessments, identify the relevant denominator or comparison population, time basis, and precision limitations. Calculate only where compatible inputs support the result; an explicitly approximate estimate or a not-determinable conclusion is acceptable, but do not manufacture exact values from rounded or bounded quantities. For the relief assessment, distinguish actions NCRAs report taking from independently verified consumer outcomes, and assess whether the excerpt supplies a usable outcome-rate denominator. Explain whether complaint volume or growth establishes the incidence of inaccuracies or violations.\n\nEnd with management-ready wording that is limited to what survives your assessments. For each use classified as Qualified or Not established, identify the specific evidence that would resolve its limitation. Keep those evidence requests confined to the four proposed uses, distinguish referenced but unavailable report material from proposed additional records, and label your recommendations as analyst judgment rather than statutory or CFPB requirements. Missing figures, response categories, report sections, or statutory provisions must not be reconstructed.",
    "full_prompt_assembly": "verbatim-natural-bridges-v1",
