    "full_prompt": "The source material provided for this request follows.\n\n[Continues under: Appendix C to Part 30—OCC Guidelines Establishing Standards for Residential Mortgage Lending Practices]\n\n2. Loan Flipping. Repeat refinancings under circumstances where the relative terms of the new and refinanced loan and the cost of the new loan do not provide a tangible economic benefit to the borrower.\n\n3. Refinancing of Special Mortgages. Refinancing of a special subsidized mortgage that contains terms favorable to the borrower with a loan that does not provide a tangible economic benefit to the borrower relative to the refinanced loan.\n\n4. Encouragement of Default. Encouraging a borrower to breach a contract and default on an existing loan prior to and in connection with the consummation of a loan that refinances all or part of the existing loan.\n\nB. Prudent Consideration of Certain Loan Terms, Conditions and Features. Certain loan terms, conditions and features, may, under particular circumstances, be susceptible to abusive, predatory, unfair or deceptive practices, yet may be appropriate and acceptable risk mitigation measures, consistent with safe and sound lending, and benefit customers under other circumstances. A national bank or Federal savings association should prudently consider the circumstances, including the characteristics of a targeted market and applicable consumer and safety and soundness safeguards, under which the national bank or Federal savings association will engage directly or indirectly in making residential mortgage loans with the following loan terms, conditions and features:\n\n1. Financing single premium credit life, disability or unemployment insurance.\n\n2. Negative amortization, involving a payment schedule in which regular periodic payments cause the principal balance to increase.\n\n3. Balloon payments in short-term transactions.\n\nUsing this material, complete the following request.\n\nDraft a prospective regulatory monitoring data specification for a national bank or Federal savings association’s residential mortgage risk team, using only the supplied Appendix C excerpt. The specification should be implementable by a data team, but it must not make findings about actual loans or institutional compliance.\n\nFirst distinguish the numbered refinancing/default practices preceding Section B from Section B’s circumstance-dependent prudent-consideration guidance. Preserve the passage numbering, the express scope of each provision, and the distinction between a potentially concerning practice and a loan feature that may be appropriate in some circumstances. The omitted introductory provisions must not be reconstructed, and the guidance must not be turned into an unconditional prohibition.\n\nSet out loan flipping, refinancing of special mortgages, encouragement of default, and the three Section B features in a source-to-data-to-test matrix. Identify the relevant source passage, the elements to be evaluated, the proposed record grain and linkage keys, minimum proposed fields with data types and missing-value treatment, and the monitoring logic those fields would support for every topic. Distinguish information needed to evaluate the passage from records or documents expressly required by it; label suggested evidence sources and provenance fields as implementation proposals rather than source-mandated documentation.\n\nDesign separate condition flags for the three practice patterns and separate feature-presence flags for Section B. Define ‘met’ as sufficient evidence for every necessary element, ‘not met’ as affirmative evidence that a necessary element is absent, and ‘indeterminate’ as insufficient evidence or an unresolved decision criterion. Apply these definitions prospectively rather than populating outcomes. Preserve the borrower and old/new-loan relationships, refinancing sequence, and timing or transactional connection needed by the relevant tests. Encouragement must not be inferred from a default alone. For negative amortization, follow the payment-schedule definition in the excerpt rather than treating any balance increase as sufficient. Identify where undefined terms or absent evaluation methods prevent a fully automated test; do not invent a repeat-count threshold, lookback period, definition of short-term, or tangible-economic-benefit formula.\n\nFor Section B, separate feature detection from the evidence of prudent consideration. Specify proposed fields that retain the relevant circumstances, targeted-market characteristics, safeguards, and assessment rationale without treating a completed checklist as proof that the feature is appropriate. Preserve the possibility of acceptable risk mitigation and customer benefit, as well as the potential for abuse under other circumstances.\n\nThen connect the two parts of the specification: show which data can be reused across tests and which comparisons or judgments must remain distinct. Explain how a refinancing transaction could generate both a practice-pattern flag and a Section B feature flag without collapsing them into one finding or treating an acceptable-feature rationale as automatic clearance of a separate concern. Derive a review-output schema from those flags that carries forward the source basis, unresolved elements, and targeted evidence requests needed to resolve indeterminate results. Any proposed review routing or ownership should be clearly identified as analyst design, not an OCC investigation or notification requirement.\n\nEnd with a bounded implementation-gap list tied to the fields and tests you specified. Separate gaps in the supplied guidance from transaction evidence that would need to be collected, and explain which downstream determinations must remain conditional until each material gap is resolved.",
