    "workflow": "Generated test",
    "task": "Exception and threshold analysis across disclosure provisions",
    "work_product": "risk-disclosure exception and threshold brief",
    "full_prompt": "The source materials provided for this request follow.\n\n[Continues under: Getting Started]\n\nYou will also want to gather and distribute other documents that your company has written for investors. It’s likely that your company has already used plain English in its glossy annual reports and other com ­ munications prepared especially for investors. These documents may save you time by showing you the type of language your company is already comfortable using. • a plain english handbook 8\n\nKnowing Your Audience\n\nKnowing your audience is the most important step in assuring that your document is understandable to your current or prospective investors. To write understandable documents, you need to gauge the financial sophistication of your investors.\n\nThrough polls and other market survey research tools, some companies know the demographics of their investors well. Other companies rely on their investor relations staff or their underwriters to describe who has bought, or is likely to buy , their securities.\n\nUsing whatever information is available, you can create a profile of your investors or prospective investors based on the following questions:\n\n• What are their demographics—age, income, level of education, and job experience?\n\n• How familiar are they with investments and financial terminology?\n\n• What investment concepts can you safely assume they understand?\n\n• How will they read the document for the first time? Will they read it straight through or skip around to the sections that interest them?\n\n• Will they read your document and your competitors’ side by side?\n\n• How will they use the document while they own the security?\n\nWhat information will they be looking for later , and is it easy to find?\n\na plain english handbook 9 “One must consider also the audience...the reader is the judge.”\n\n---\n\n[Continues under: The Elements of Style]\n\nWe provide information to you about our notes in three separate documents that progressively provide more detail: 1) the prospectus,\n\n2) the prospectus supplement, and 3) the pricing supplement. Since the terms of speciﬁc notes may differ from the general information we have provided, in all cases rely on information in the pricing supplement over different information in the prospectus and the prospectus supplement; and rely on this prospectus supplement over different information in the prospectus.\n\n28 a plain english handbook or We provide information to you about our notes in three separate documents that progressively provide more detail: 1 The Prospectus General information that may or may not apply to each note.\n\n2 The Prospectus Supplement\n\nMore speciﬁc than the prospectus, and to the extent information differs from the prospectus, rely on the different information in this document.\n\n3 The Pricing Supplement\n\nProvides ﬁnal details about a speciﬁc note including its price. To the extent information differs from the prospectus or the prospectus supplement, rely on the different information in this document.\n\nUsing these materials, complete the following request.\n\nProvide a risk-disclosure exception and threshold brief for a disclosure committee designing a prospective review of a note disclosure package. Rely exclusively on the supplied handbook passages. Distinguish audience-oriented drafting guidance and illustrative disclosure language from binding legal requirements; do not make findings about an actual issuer. Present “threshold” as the qualitative condition for changing which information a reader should rely on, without adding a numerical or materiality test.\n\nReconstruct the applicability and reliance logic across the prospectus, prospectus supplement and pricing supplement. Identify, in a decision table, each document relationship, the condition that activates priority, the information affected by that priority, and what remains unresolved without the actual note documents. Address general information whose applicability to a particular note is uncertain, differing information between document layers, and additional detail for which no difference has been established. Assess whether the narrative and numbered versions of the example support the same conclusions, paying particular attention to whether priority displaces an entire document or only particular information. Anchor material conclusions to short source quotations.\n\nUse that analysis together with the audience guidance to recommend how the review should test whether investors can find and understand the applicable information. Connect each proposed presentation control to a reliance or applicability issue from your table and to a relevant audience consideration, such as familiarity with investment terminology, reading straight through versus skipping between sections, or later retrieval while holding the security. Draft a short reader-facing explanation of the hierarchy, then explain how its presentation might change depending on the audience information obtained. Keep those changes conditional because no actual investor profile is supplied, and label proposed controls and drafting choices as analyst recommendations rather than handbook mandates.\n\nClose with a targeted evidence-gap and disposition table limited to note-specific applicability, differences between the three document layers, and the audience characteristics needed for your presentation recommendations. For each gap, identify the document or information that would resolve it and the conclusion or recommendation that must remain conditional until then. Distinguish documents mentioned in the example from evidence you propose collecting, including any investor-research or existing investor-communication materials. A document must not be inferred to be legally required merely because the handbook refers to it, and missing case evidence must not be treated as proof of a disclosure failure.",
