    "workflow": "Generated test",
    "task": "Exception and threshold analysis across disclosure provisions",
    "work_product": "risk-disclosure exception and threshold brief",
    "full_prompt": "The source materials provided for this request follow.\n\n[Continues under: Information You Need]\n\n• Is any important information missing?\n\n• Does the document include information that is not legally required and will not help investors make informed decisions?\n\nFor time-saving tips on how to outline and reorganize your document, read Chapter 8 on page 55.\n\na plain english handbook 11 “Writers must therefore constantly ask: What am I trying to say?\n\nSurprisingly often they don’t know. They must look at what they have written and ask: Have I said it?”\n\nWilliam Zinsser\n\nOn Writing Well\n\nMeet to resolve questions Meet with the authors of the original document or others who under­ stand it and any members of the team who can help to answer the questions you wrote in the margins. Besides the obvious reason for the meeting, another more important goal is to question the need for everything that appears in the document.\n\n“Because it’s always been there” is not reason enough to keep it in your draf t. Since much of the language in these documents is recycled from older (or another company’s) documents, often no one knows who initially wrote it or why it is needed now. If you’ve done your legal research and no one knows why the information is important or required, consider taking it out.\n\nEliminate redundant information Question the need for repeating any information. Reading the same material two or three times can bore and even trouble readers. Most readers skip over paragraphs if they think they’ve read them before.\n\nIf you cut down on repetitious paragraphs or sentences, you’ll not only earn the gratitude of your reader, you’ll reduce printing and mailing costs.\n\n---\n\n[Continues under: Robin Williams, The Mac Is Not a Typewriter]\n\nTo highlight information and maintain readability, use a different size or weight of your typeface. T ry using extra white space, bold type, shading, rules, boxes, or sidebars in the margins to make information stand out. In this handbook we use dotted rules to highlight the exam­ ples. Whatever method you choose to highlight information, use it consistently throughout your document so your readers can recognize how you flag important information.\n\nTHE SECURITIES AND EXCHANGE COMMISSION HAS\n\nNOT APPROVED OR DISAPPROVED THESE SECURITIES\n\nOR DETERMINED IF THIS PROSPECTUS IS TRUTHFUL OR\n\nCOMPLETE. ANY REPRESENTATION TO THE CONTRARY\n\nThe Securities and Exchange Commission has not approved or disapproved these securities or determined if this prospectus is truthful or complete. Any representation to the contrary is a criminal offense.\n\n“...words consisting of only capital letters present the most difficult reading— because of their equal height, equal volume, and, with most, their equal width.”\n\nUsing these materials, complete the following request.\n\nProvide a risk-disclosure exception and threshold brief for disclosure counsel developing a prospective standard for streamlining prospectus language. Rely solely on the supplied plain-English handbook passages. This is a proposed review standard, not a compliance finding about a particular issuer.\n\nSet out the connection between the content-retention guidance and the presentation guidance in a decision table. Identify the trigger, the source passage using a short quotation, any prerequisite or qualification, the action the guidance supports, and what remains unresolved for every decision point. Address information’s importance, legal necessity and usefulness to investors; inherited language with an uncertain purpose; repetition; and readable, consistent emphasis. Preserve the distinction between questioning whether text is needed, considering its removal, and having sufficient support to approve an edit. Where the source supplies no final approval threshold or express exception, say so. Suggested practices must not be turned into binding disclosure duties, and numerical cutoffs or deadlines must not be invented.\n\nUse that analysis to construct a review sequence in which the content decision determines whether the reviewer proceeds to removal, consolidation, further inquiry, or presentation-only changes. Explain how unresolved legal necessity or investor usefulness affects the next step. Identify which parts of this sequence follow directly from the handbook and which are conservative safeguards you recommend as analyst judgment; content and presentation concerns may overlap.\n\nStress-test the sequence against the uppercase and sentence-case SEC warning examples in the presentation passage. Assess whether difficult typography or apparently repeated substance, by itself, supports deleting any warning content. Distinguish alternative highlighting techniques from the guidance that applies once a highlighting method is selected. Compare the wording actually visible in the two examples, but do not assume the uppercase excerpt is complete, treat the handbook’s demonstrations as duplicate disclosures in an issuer’s prospectus, or infer that the illustrated warning is legally required in a particular filing.\n\nEnd with a conditional editing recommendation derived from the earlier decision gates: what could proceed as a presentation change, what would remain unresolved before a substantive cut, and what narrowly targeted follow-up evidence would resolve those uncertainties. Limit those requests to legal necessity, investor usefulness, completeness of the proposed wording, and consistency of the proposed emphasis. Distinguish handbook-stated review steps from evidence or approvals you recommend, and do not fill gaps using outside securities law or the unsupplied Chapter 8.",
