    "workflow": "Generated test",
    "task": "Policy requirement traceability across disclosure provisions",
    "work_product": "risk-disclosure policy traceability matrix",
    "full_prompt": "The source materials provided for this request follow.\n\nA Plain English\n\nHandbook\n\nHow to create clear SEC disclosure documents By the Office of Investor Education and Assistance\n\nU.S. Securities and Exchange Commission\n\n450 5th Street, N.W\n\nWashington, DC 20549\n\nAugust 1998\n\nThis handbook shows how you can use well-established techniques for writing in plain English to create clearer and more informative disclosure documents. We are publishing this handbook only for your general information. Of course, when drafting a document for filing with the SEC, you must make sure it meets all legal requirements.\n\nAcknowledgments\n\nThis handbook reflects the work, ideas, and generosity of many individuals and organizations at the SEC and in the private sector.\n\nAt the SEC, staff in the Divisions of Corporation Finance and\n\nInvestment M\n\nanagement, the Offices of Public Affairs and General Counsel, and the Chairman’s Office provided insightful comments.\n\nIn particular, Commissioner Isaac C. Hunt Jr., Nick Balamaci, Barry\n\nBarbash, Gregg Corso, Brian Lane, Diane Sanger, Jennifer Scardino, Michael Schlein, Heidi Stam, and Tony Vertuno offered invaluable advice and guidance.\n\nCorporate officials and lawyers enthusiastically helped us to breathe life into our plain English initiatives and this handbook. The S ociety of Corporate Secretaries, the American Bar Association, and The Bond Market Association invited us to conduct workshops where we tested much of the information in the handbook. Kathleen Gibson, Peggy Foran, Susan Wolf, Bruce Bennett, Jim McKenzie, Jeff Klauder, Fred Green, Mark Howard, Pierre de Saint Phalle, Richard M. Phillips, and Alan J. Davis contributed mightily to our efforts.\n\n---\n\n[Continues under: The Elements of Style]\n\nWe provide information to you about our notes in three separate documents that progressively provide more detail: 1) the prospectus,\n\n2) the prospectus supplement, and 3) the pricing supplement. Since the terms of speciﬁc notes may differ from the general information we have provided, in all cases rely on information in the pricing supplement over different information in the prospectus and the prospectus supplement; and rely on this prospectus supplement over different information in the prospectus.\n\n28 a plain english handbook or We provide information to you about our notes in three separate documents that progressively provide more detail: 1 The Prospectus General information that may or may not apply to each note.\n\n2 The Prospectus Supplement\n\nMore speciﬁc than the prospectus, and to the extent information differs from the prospectus, rely on the different information in this document.\n\n3 The Pricing Supplement\n\nProvides ﬁnal details about a speciﬁc note including its price. To the extent information differs from the prospectus or the prospectus supplement, rely on the different information in this document.\n\n---\n\n[Continues under: Entire Prospectus—Design]\n\n(d) ( ) To enhance the readability of the prospectus, you must use plain English principles in the organization, language, and design of the front and back cover pages, the summary, and the risk factors section.\n\n(2) You must draft the language in these sections so that at a mini­ mum it substantially complies with each of the following plain English writing principles:\n\n(ii) Definite, concrete, everyday words;\n\n(iii) Active voice;\n\n(iv) Tabular presentation or bullet lists for complex material,\n\n(v) No legal jargon or highly technical business terms; and\n\n(vi) No multiple negatives.\n\n(3) In designing these sections or other sections of the prospectus, you may include pictures, logos, charts, graphs, or other design elements so long as the design is not misleading and the required information is clear . You are encouraged to use tables, schedules, charts and graphic illustrations of the results of operations, balance sheet, or other financial data that present the data in an understandable manner. Any presentation must be consistent with the financial statements and non-financial information in the prospectus. You must draw the graphs and charts to scale. Any information you provide must not be misleading.\n\nUsing these materials, complete the following request.\n\nFor our disclosure-control team, prepare a risk-disclosure policy traceability matrix based solely on the supplied excerpts from the SEC’s August 1998 A Plain English Handbook. The matrix should support a prospective drafting and review policy, not make issuer-specific compliance findings or certify the completeness of current legal requirements.\n\nThe matrix must include columns for the source anchor, nature and force of the passage, applicable document or section, condition or qualification, proposed policy check and acceptance basis, and review evidence or unresolved limitation. Cite the supplied heading, any legible clause label, and a short supporting quotation for each substantive row. Where passages differ in operative force or scope, split them; missing numbering or text must not be reconstructed.\n\nUse the introductory general-information caveat to establish the authority boundary for the whole matrix, while preserving the mandatory wording actually reproduced in the disclosure provision. Distinguish mandatory wording, conditional permission, encouragement or guidance, and illustrative drafting language. Trace the plain-English duty to the covered prospectus sections and the writing principles actually visible in the excerpt, retaining the source’s qualified compliance standard. Separately map the permission to use design elements, its conditions, the encouragement concerning understandable financial presentations, and the mandatory presentation restrictions. Clearly distinguish which checks concern the named sections and which design provisions also address other prospectus sections; those duties must not be extended to supplements solely because supplements appear elsewhere in the handbook.\n\nIntegrate the three-document note example into the same matrix as a conditional reader-reliance check. Derive the hierarchy from the alternative presentations without counting them as separate obligations, and preserve the conditions governing general note information and differing information. Do not convert this illustration into a universal filing requirement or an obligation to make every document’s terms identical. Explain what would need to be established before our team could use that hierarchy for an actual offering.\n\nThen use the completed matrix to propose a brief reviewer decision path for a future offering that adopts the illustrated hierarchy and includes a chart in its prospectus risk-factors section. Show how differing note information would affect reader reliance, which applicable language and design checks would still need review, and where an unresolved applicability or evidence question would prevent a conclusion. Link those decisions back to the relevant matrix entries rather than providing an unrelated summary.\n\nProposed review evidence and workflow steps must be labeled as analyst recommendations rather than source-mandated records or procedures. Bound the gap assessment to authority, scope, source coverage, and evidence needed for the matrix’s checks. Leave missing provision text and actual issuer compliance unknown, and do not introduce numerical acceptance thresholds, external legal rules, or an effective date inferred from the handbook’s publication date.",
