    "full_prompt": "The source material provided for this request follows.\n\n[Continues under: Appendix D to Part 30—OCC Guidelines Establishing Heightened Standards for Certain Large Insured National Banks, Insured Federal Savings Associations, and Insured Federal Branches]\n\n10. Risk appetite means the aggregate level and types of risk the board of directors and management are willing to assume to achieve a covered bank's strategic objectives and business plan, consistent with applicable capital, liquidity, and other regulatory requirements.\n\n11. Risk profile means a point-in-time assessment of a covered bank's risks, aggregated within and across each relevant risk category, using methodologies consistent with the risk appetite statement described in paragraph II.E. of these Guidelines.\n\nA. Risk Governance Framework. A covered bank should establish and adhere to a formal, written risk governance framework that is designed by independent risk management and approved by the board of directors or the board's risk committee. The risk governance framework should include delegations of authority from the board of directors to management committees and executive officers as well as the risk limits established for material activities. Independent risk management should review and update the risk governance framework at least annually, and as often as needed to address improvements in industry risk management practices and changes in the covered bank's risk profile caused by emerging risks, its strategic plans, or other internal and external factors.\n\nB. Scope of Risk Governance Framework. The risk governance framework should cover the following risk categories that apply to the covered bank: Credit risk, interest rate risk, liquidity risk, price risk, operational risk, compliance risk, strategic risk, and reputation risk.\n\nUsing this material, complete the following request.\n\nProvide a compliance-response option brief for a covered bank’s board risk committee based solely on the supplied definitions 10–11 and Sections A–B of Appendix D. This is a prospective implementation analysis: no actual bank framework, risk assessment or review history has been supplied, so do not make an institution-specific compliance finding.\n\nBegin with a concise source-to-decision crosswalk covering framework design and approval, delegations and material-activity limits, review and update responsibilities and timing, and risk-category scope. The definitions of risk appetite and risk profile must remain distinct, with their qualifications preserved. Explain how that distinction informs an assessment of whether changes in the risk profile call for additional framework maintenance. Preserve both the annual cadence and the qualified as-needed standard rather than treating either as a substitute for the other. Cite the relevant definition or section beside each standard, and do not reconstruct the missing contents of paragraph II.E.\n\nCompare these analyst-proposed response options: retain the existing framework design with ongoing review and updating; make targeted compliance-risk amendments while retaining the rest of the framework; or undertake broader framework redesign across applicable risk categories. These are planning alternatives, not remedies prescribed by the excerpt. Identify, for each option, the factual conditions under which it could be sufficient, the conditions that would make it insufficient, its principal limitation, and the crosswalk entries supporting that assessment. Distinguish responsibilities expressly assigned by the source from any implementation responsibilities you recommend. A requirement for board approval of every subsequent update must not be inferred from the framework-approval provision.\n\nMake the scope comparison substantive: explain why a targeted remediation effort is not necessarily the same as a compliance-risk-only framework. Identify the listed risk categories and retain the qualification that coverage concerns categories applicable to the bank; neither assume every category applies nor assume unverified categories can be omitted. Assess each option conditionally where applicability or the existing framework’s quality is unknown.\n\nConclude with a conditional recommendation path derived from the option comparison that distinguishes evidence supporting retention of the design, a localized gap suitable for amendment, broader gaps warranting redesign as analyst judgment, and insufficient evidence to choose. Allow responses to be sequenced where appropriate. For each branch, specify what evidence would establish or defeat its preconditions and how that result would change the recommended response. Bound the evidence requests to the crosswalk’s governance, authority/limits, maintenance and category-scope questions. Separate the formal written framework expressly called for by the excerpt from corroborating materials you propose requesting, and do not treat missing bank evidence as proof of a deficiency or invent remediation deadlines.",
