    "full_prompt": "The source material provided for this request follows.\n\n[Continues under: Appendix C to Part 30—OCC Guidelines Establishing Standards for Residential Mortgage Lending Practices]\n\n3. Standards related to total loan compensation and total compensation of intermediaries, including maximum rates, points, and other charges, and the use of overages and yield-spread premiums, structured to avoid providing an incentive to originate loans with predatory or abusive characteristics.\n\n4. Requirements for agreements with intermediaries and originators, including with respect to risks identified in the due diligence process, compliance with appropriate national bank or Federal savings association policies, procedures and practices and with applicable law (including remedies for failure to comply), protection of the national bank or Federal savings association against risk, and termination procedures.\n\n5. Loan documentation procedures, management information systems, quality control reviews, and other methods through which the national bank or Federal savings association will verify compliance with agreements, bank or savings association policies, and applicable laws, and otherwise retain appropriate oversight of mortgage origination functions, including loan sourcing, underwriting, and loan closings.\n\n6. Criteria and procedures for the national bank or Federal savings association to take appropriate corrective action, including modification of loan terms and termination of the relationship with the intermediary or originator in question.\n\nUsing this material, complete the following request.\n\nFor a reviewer planning an assessment of residential mortgage origination relationships with intermediaries and originators at a national bank or Federal savings association, provide a compliance review evidence specification brief. Rely solely on the supplied items 3–6 of Appendix C to Part 30. Treat this as a prospective review specification: no institution records or actual compliance findings have been supplied, and the excerpt is not the complete appendix.\n\nSet out links from each review objective to its numbered item and a short supporting passage in a traceability table. Distinguish, for every objective, the agreement, procedure, system, or other mechanism expressly identified in the excerpt from the particular evidence artifacts you recommend requesting. Specify separately what evidence could establish control design and what additional evidence could demonstrate operation. Label proposed records and testing methods as reviewer judgment, not additional source requirements.\n\nIn the table, preserve the distinctions that matter for testing: total loan compensation versus total compensation of intermediaries; compensation limits versus the incentive structure; agreement provisions addressing due-diligence risks, compliance, remedies, protection against risk, and termination; verification against agreements, institutional policies, and applicable laws; and oversight across loan sourcing, underwriting, and closings. Keep contractual remedies, loan-term modification, and relationship termination distinct rather than treating them as interchangeable responses.\n\nUse the table to develop linked testing paths for a potential compensation-incentive concern and a risk identified through due diligence. Explain how each concern could be traced into agreement provisions, tested through the verification mechanisms in item 5, and evaluated against the corrective-action criteria and procedures in item 6. These are hypothetical review paths, not asserted events. Cite the items supporting each link and identify connections that are your review-design recommendations rather than relationships expressly prescribed by the text. Explain how an unresolved upstream criterion or missing agreement would limit the downstream verification or corrective-action assessment.\n\nFor the proposed tests, state what would support the specified review criterion, what evidence would indicate a gap against that criterion, and when the result must remain undetermined. Distinguish an unspecified criterion in the excerpt from institution evidence that has simply not been provided. Do not treat missing records in this exercise as proof of a control failure, staying within a compensation limit as sufficient proof of appropriate incentives, or a detected problem as automatically requiring termination. Do not invent numerical limits, definitions of predatory or abusive characteristics, applicable-law contents, sampling thresholds, or corrective-action triggers; identify any additional criterion needed before a conclusive test could be performed.\n\nEnd with a targeted evidence-request plan derived from the testing paths. Bound it to compensation design and operation, due-diligence risks and agreements, origination verification, and corrective-action criteria and records. Sequence requests according to which downstream assessments they enable, distinguish necessary test inputs from optional corroboration, and explain which conclusions would remain conditional even after the proposed records were obtained.",
