    "full_prompt": "The source material provided for this request follows.\n\n[Continues under: The Elements of Style]\n\nFor example: negative compound single word not able unable not accept reject not certain uncertain not unlike similar, alike does not have lacks does not include excludes, omits not many few not often rarely not the same different not … unless only if not … except only if not … until only when a plain english handbook 27 “There’s not much to be said about the period except that most writers don’t reach it soon enough.”\n\nUse short sentences No one likes to read a sentence that’s two pages long. And yet, lengthy, information-packed sentences choke many prospectuses today. To complicate matters further, these sentences are filled with jargon and legalese. The longer and more complex a sentence, the harder it is for readers to understand any single portion of it.\n\nbefore The following description encompasses all the material terms and provisions of the Notes offered hereby and supplements, and to the extent inconsistent therewith replaces, the description of the general terms and provisions of the Debt Securities (as defined in the accompanying Prospectus) set forth under the heading “Description of Debt Securities” in the Prospectus, to which description reference is hereby made. The following description will apply to each Note unless otherwise specified in the applicable Pricing Supplement.\n\nIf you really want to root out the problem with this paragraph, you need to think of the deeper reasons why it doesn ’t work. If you look beyond the language used, you’ll find that it presents complex information without first providing a context for the reader.\n\nThe rewrites that follow show two ways to provide the context, with and without tabulation.\n\nUsing this material, complete the following request.\n\nProvide securities counsel with a source-grounded disclosure compliance brief for developing a prospective drafting-review standard from the supplied plain-English handbook excerpt. Use no external securities-law rules. Treat the quoted “before” paragraph as illustrative language, not evidence about an issuer or an actual offering, and explain the limits of what this excerpt establishes about mandatory disclosure requirements.\n\nSet out a concise mapping that links relevant source passages to their evidentiary status, drafting implications, and proposed review checks. Distinguish handbook guidance from representations made within the illustrative paragraph, and label your own acceptance criteria as reviewer judgment rather than source requirements. Connect the advice about short sentences with the concerns about jargon, legalese, and providing reader context; do not invent a numerical sentence-length limit.\n\nApply that mapping by rewriting the illustrative Notes paragraph in shorter, context-first sentences. Preserve the scope of its assertion about material terms, the relationship between the current description and the accompanying Prospectus, the conditional effect of an inconsistency, the defined-term and named-heading references, and the application to individual Notes subject to the Pricing Supplement exception. The contents of the referenced documents must not be inferred, and the rewrites mentioned but not included in the excerpt must not be claimed to support your wording. Follow the rewrite with a compact fidelity check tracing these substantive features to the original language and identifying any ambiguity your revision cannot resolve.\n\nEnd with a conditional readiness assessment for using the revision as a drafting template rather than a certification of legal compliance. Base that assessment on the mapping and fidelity check: distinguish presentation checks assessable from the excerpt from substantive checks that remain unknown. Limit the latter to material-term coverage, cross-reference accuracy, interaction between the descriptions, and Note-specific exceptions. Identify the referenced material or additional analyst-proposed evidence needed for every unresolved check, and explain what drafting decision it would inform. Clearly distinguish documents merely referenced in the example from anything expressly required by the handbook; do not turn proposed review evidence into a filing or record-retention obligation.",
